{
  "dom_id": "step-q36523",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Consider that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first, second and third-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ What is the value of ${f}_{yxy}(1,0)-{f}_{yy}(1,0)-{f}_{xyy}(1,0)$ given that ${f}_{yy}(x,y)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy?$",
  "worked_solution": "As the function and its first, second, and third-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ we must have ${f}_{yxy}(x,y)={f}_{xyy}(x,y).$ \n\nTherefore, we can swap the order of the subscripts in the first term. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxy}(1,0)-{f}_{yy}(1,0)-{f}_{xyy}(1,0) & ={f}_{xyy}(1,0)-{f}_{yy}(1,0)-{f}_{xyy}(1,0) \\\\  & =\\cancel{{f}_{xyy}(1,0)}-{f}_{yy}(1,0)-\\cancel{{f}_{xyy}(1,0)} \\\\  & =-{f}_{yy}(1,0) \\\\  & =-(1)^{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
